    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Cheap treadmills fail for the same reasons expensive ones do — heat, friction, dirt and
          overload — but they reach the failure point sooner, because every part is sized with less
          margin. A premium machine can shrug off a dry deck or a heavy user for a while; a $250 walking
          pad cannot. That makes the owner's habits matter more on a budget machine than on any other.
          This guide sets out the nine that make the biggest difference, in rough order of how much
          they matter, and explains why each one works on a small motor and a short belt.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How to Make a Cheap Treadmill Last Longer: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Keep a budget treadmill well inside its weight and speed ratings, give its small
            motor shorter sessions with rests, and keep friction low with regular lubrication and
            correct belt tension.</strong> Those three habits address heat, which is what ends most
            budget motors and controller boards.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest are supporting habits: plug it directly into a grounded wall outlet rather than a
            power strip or extension cord, put it on a level, hard, clean surface with air around the
            motor, keep dust and pet hair out from under the belt, and store and move it the way the
            manual says. Register it on the day it arrives so the warranty is there when a part does
            fail.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this turns a $250 walking pad into a gym machine. Nobody publishes reliable lifespan
            data for budget pads, and a one-year motor warranty tells you what the maker is willing to
            stand behind. But the gap between a pad that is looked after and one that is run hot, dry and
            overloaded is large, and almost all of it is within your control. If you have not bought yet,
            our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>
            names machines whose figures held up and explains what this budget can and cannot buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-cheap-wears" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Why Budget Machines Wear Out Sooner</h2>
<p>
            It helps to know what is different inside a cheap machine, because each difference points to
            a habit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor is smaller than the box says.</strong> Budget pads are sold with headline
            figures of 2.25 to 3 HP. Where independent coverage has read the manual or the motor label,
            the continuous rating has come out somewhere around 0.6 to 0.8 HP. That is enough to walk on,
            but it leaves little in reserve, so anything that adds load — a heavier user, a dry deck, an
            over-tight belt, a long session — pushes it towards its limit, and a motor near its limit runs
            hot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The electronics carry the load.</strong> The motor control board converts mains power
            into what the motor needs and is often the first thing to fail on a budget machine. It suffers
            from the same heat as the motor, and from poor power supply.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Everything mechanical is small.</strong> Short belts, thin decks, small rollers and
            light frames keep the machine cheap and easy to slide under a sofa. Small rollers turn faster
            and flex the belt more sharply; a thin deck flexes more under each step; a light frame is
            more sensitive to an uneven floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The warranty is short.</strong> At this price a year is typical, sometimes with an
            extension for registering the machine. When the maker is only willing to cover the motor for a
            year, every month beyond that is up to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The common thread is heat from friction and overload. Every habit below reduces one or the
            other. Our full
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a>
            covers the complete schedule for any treadmill; this page concentrates on what matters most
            when the machine has no margin to spare.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Stay Well Inside the Rated Capacity and Speed</h2>
<p>
            The printed weight capacity is a ceiling the manufacturer will stand behind, not a comfortable
            operating point. On a budget machine, where the figure itself is often inconsistent between
            the listing and the manual, it deserves even less trust.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leave a margin of at least 50 lb.</strong> A pad rated at 265 lb is at its limit with
            a 265 lb walker on it, every step. Each extra pound means more belt-to-deck friction and more
            current through the motor and board. If the listing and the manual disagree on capacity, use
            the lower figure. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains why running loads the deck with far more than body weight, which is the other reason
            the margin matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Count everyone who uses it.</strong> A pad bought for one person often becomes the
            household's machine. Size the margin for the heaviest regular user, and remember that a
            weighted vest or a backpack adds to the load the same way body weight does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not live at top speed.</strong> On a 2-in-1 pad that reaches 6.2 mph with the bar
            raised, the top speed is a short jog, not a training pace. Running near the top of a small
            motor's range for long periods asks it for sustained power it was not sized for. Some brands
            even rate their pads for a lower weight at running speed than at walking speed. Use the
            jogging mode for a few minutes at the end of a walk, not as the session.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking machines are for walking.</strong> If you find yourself running on a pad most
            days, the machine is the wrong one for you, and no maintenance habit will change that. Our
            page on whether you
            <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">can run on a walking pad</a>
            sets out the limits plainly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="session-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Rest the Motor: Session Length and Cool-Down</h2>
<p>
            This is the habit most specific to budget machines, and the one most often ignored, because
            walking pads are marketed for all-day use under a desk.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heat builds with time, not just effort.</strong> A small motor at steady load gets
            warmer the longer it runs, and so does the board driving it. A short session lets it shed that
            heat; a five-hour stint at a desk may not. Slow walking is not automatically gentle on a motor
            either: at low speed under load, a motor has to deliver torque, and torque is current.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check your manual for a session limit.</strong> Some budget pads' manuals or listings
            state a maximum continuous running time followed by a rest period. If yours does, treat it as
            a hard limit. If it does not, a sensible default for a budget pad used at a desk is to break
            long days into blocks with genuine pauses between them — step off, stop the belt and let the
            machine sit — rather than running it from nine to five.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slow down before you stop.</strong> Ending a session with a minute or two at a gentle
            pace is good for you and lets the machine wind down from its working load before the belt
            stops. Then leave it to cool before folding it, covering it or sliding it under furniture,
            where heat has nowhere to go.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not fight a thermal cut-out.</strong> If the machine stops by itself when warm and
            restarts after a rest, it is protecting itself. Restarting it again and again to the cut-out
            point ages the motor and board faster. Treat the first cut-out as a signal to check the deck,
            belt tension and dust before the next session; our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill overheating</a>
            sets out the causes in order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Feel and smell are your gauges.</strong> A motor area that is warm after a long session
            is normal. One that smells of hot electrics or scorched plastic is not: stop, unplug at the
            wall and do not restart until you know why.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lubrication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Lubricate the Deck on Schedule</h2>
<p>
            The belt slides over the deck under your weight on every step, and the lubricant between them
            is what keeps that friction low. When it dries out, friction climbs, the motor draws more
            current to hold speed, and everything runs hotter. A dry deck is the most common cause of a
            budget machine running hot or failing early, and it is the cheapest problem on this page to
            prevent.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use what the manual specifies.</strong> Most budget pads and treadmills call for
            100% silicone treadmill lubricant, and many ship with a small bottle. Do not use household
            sprays or oils that are not designed for the purpose; our page on whether you
            <a href="/can-you-use-wd40-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">can use WD-40 on a treadmill</a>
            explains why not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Follow the interval, and keep a note.</strong> Manuals usually give an interval by
            hours of use or by months. A pad used for hours every working day reaches an hours-based
            interval far sooner than one used for a weekend walk, so a desk walker should check more
            often. Write the date on a strip of tape under the deck or in your phone, because nobody
            remembers when they last did it. Several budget brands make lubrication a condition of the
            warranty, so the note is also your evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do it properly.</strong> Unplug, lift the belt edge, apply the amount the manual says
            across the centre of the deck where your feet land, then walk slowly for a few minutes to
            spread it. Too much is not better: excess lubricant can make the belt slip and attract dust.
            Our step-by-step guide to
            <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricating a walking pad</a>
            takes about ten minutes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know the signs of a dry deck.</strong> A belt that hesitates under your step, a motor
            that sounds as if it is working harder, a slightly warm smell after a session that never used
            to produce one, or a belt that feels rough or dry when you slide a hand underneath it (with the
            machine unplugged) all point the same way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tension-tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Set Belt Tension and Tracking Correctly</h2>
<p>
            Belt tension is a balance, and on a small motor both sides of it hurt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Too tight</strong> presses the belt hard onto the deck and the rollers, raising
            friction, loading the roller bearings and making the motor work harder for the same speed.
            It is a common mistake because tightening is the instinctive response to a slipping belt,
            when the real cause of slip is often a dry deck. Lubricate first, then adjust.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Too loose</strong> lets the belt slip on the front roller under each step, which
            wears the belt and roller and makes the speed surge. It also lets the belt wander.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Adjust in small, equal steps.</strong> Most budget machines have two hex bolts at the
            rear roller. Turn both by the same amount — a quarter-turn at a time — with the machine
            unplugged between checks, then run it slowly to test. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>
            explains the lift test most manuals describe.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep it centred.</strong> A belt that drifts to one side rubs the frame, frays its
            edge and loads one end of the rollers more than the other. Small tracking corrections are
            normal maintenance, especially in the first weeks while a new belt beds in. Make them with the
            belt running slowly and empty, by turning one rear bolt a small amount, and never reach near
            the roller while it moves. If the belt drifts constantly, check that the machine is level
            and that you are walking in the centre — a pad under a desk often drifts because the walker
            stands slightly to one side to reach the keyboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Give It Clean, Direct Power</h2>
<p>
            The motor control board is the most expensive part of most budget machines relative to the
            price, and power problems are one of the things that kill it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plug directly into a grounded wall outlet.</strong> A treadmill motor draws a surge of
            current when it starts and a heavy, varying load while it runs. Ordinary power strips and
            household extension cords are not built for that, and a long or thin cord can starve the motor
            of voltage, which makes it run hotter. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cord and circuit guide</a>
            explains why, and what to do if the outlet is in the wrong place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Avoid sharing a busy circuit.</strong> A treadmill on the same circuit as a space
            heater, a microwave or a vacuum can see voltage dips when those switch on. If lights dim when
            the belt starts, or the breaker trips, the circuit is telling you something.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Follow the manual on surge protection.</strong> Some manufacturers recommend a surge
            protector, some specify a type, and a few say not to use one; the manual decides. What is
            almost never right is an ordinary multi-outlet strip.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Switch off and unplug when it is not in use.</strong> Turning the machine off at its
            own switch, removing the safety key and unplugging it when you are done for the day protects
            the electronics from surges, stops children starting it, and avoids leaving the board powered
            for no reason. It also means the machine is already unplugged when you come to clean or
            lubricate it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Place It Where It Can Breathe</h2>
<p>
            Where a budget machine lives affects how hard it works and how much dirt it swallows.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Level and hard.</strong> A light frame on an uneven floor twists slightly, which makes
            the belt track badly and loads the rollers unevenly. Put it on a flat, hard surface, and check
            with a spirit level if the belt keeps drifting. A firm treadmill mat helps on hard floors and is
            close to essential on carpet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Not directly on carpet.</strong> Carpet fibres and the dust in them are drawn into the
            motor area by the cooling airflow and under the belt by its motion. They also block air under
            low-profile pads. If the machine has to go on carpet, a mat underneath reduces both problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Air around the motor.</strong> The motor and board need to shed heat. Keep vents and
            the motor end clear of walls, curtains and furniture while the machine runs, and do not run a
            pad with something stored against it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A sensible room.</strong> Damp, very cold or very hot spaces are hard on electronics
            and on lubricant. Garages and unheated outbuildings can work, but they bring condensation,
            dust and temperature swings, and many garage outlets are GFCI-protected, which some treadmills
            are not designed to run on. Our guide to keeping a
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill in a garage</a>
            covers the precautions if that is the only space you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Keep Dust, Sweat and Hair Out</h2>
<p>
            Dirt is friction in a different form. It gets between the belt and deck, into the roller
            bearings and, on machines with an open motor area, onto the motor and board, where it holds
            heat in.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wipe after use.</strong> Sweat is corrosive and sticky. A quick wipe of the frame,
            console or remote, and the belt surface with a damp cloth (machine unplugged) stops it
            building up. Avoid harsh cleaners and do not spray liquid into the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vacuum around and under it weekly.</strong> Pads sit low and act as dust collectors.
            Slide the machine aside, or lift it if it is light enough, and vacuum the floor beneath it.
            Pet hair is a particular problem: it wraps around rollers and gets drawn under the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean under the belt occasionally.</strong> With the machine unplugged and the belt
            loosened if the manual says so, a lint-free cloth slid under the belt removes the grey paste of
            dust and old lubricant that builds up on the deck. Lubricate afterwards. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning a treadmill</a>
            has the full routine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor area, if you are allowed.</strong> On a folding treadmill with a removable hood,
            vacuuming the motor compartment gently every few months is good practice. On a sealed walking
            pad, do not open the casing unless the manual tells you how; on some budget machines it is a
            warranty question as well as a safety one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Store It Properly, and Register It</h2>
<p>
            The last two habits are about the times the machine is not running.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Store it the way it was designed to be stored.</strong> Some pads are designed to stand
            on end; many are not, and standing one up can put load on parts never meant to carry it or let
            lubricant run. Folding treadmills should be folded and locked using the mechanism, not left
            half-folded. Let the machine cool before you fold or cover it. If it lives under a bed or sofa,
            clean the space first, because it will collect dust there between sessions. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-store-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">storing a treadmill</a>
            covers longer-term storage too.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Move it gently.</strong> Use the transport wheels, keep the machine level when
            carrying it up or down steps, and never lift it by the console, the fold-up bar or the remote
            holder. Dropping a pad on its end can crack a deck or knock a roller out of line.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look after the small parts.</strong> On a walking pad, the remote is often the only
            way to control the machine. Keep it charged or with fresh batteries, keep it out of reach of
            children and pets, and check before you buy that the brand sells a replacement. The safety key
            matters too; keep it attached when walking.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Register the machine on day one.</strong> Some budget brands extend their warranty for
            registration, and all of them want proof of purchase. Register it, keep the order number,
            photograph the serial label, and keep a simple note of lubrication dates. When a part fails in
            month ten, that paperwork decides whether the fix is free.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Know when it is done.</strong> Even with good habits, budget machines reach a point
            where a repair costs more than they are worth. A motor control board or motor out of warranty
            on a $250 pad is usually that point. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            sets out the repair-or-replace arithmetic, and what fails in what order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. If You Are Still Shopping for a Budget Machine</h2>
<p>
            The easiest way to make a cheap treadmill last is to choose one that has a chance to. If you
            have not bought yet, a few things at the buying stage make every habit above easier.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Capacity that matches the manual,</strong> with 50 lb or more of margin over the
            heaviest regular user.</li>
            <li><strong>A brand with its own store,</strong> written warranty terms and replacement parts —
            remotes, belts, safety keys — that you can buy outside the marketplace.</li>
            <li><strong>A belt long enough for you,</strong> so you walk in the centre rather than near the
            ends, which is kinder to the belt and safer.</li>
            <li><strong>A clear lubrication instruction</strong> in the manual, and ideally lubricant in the
            box.</li>
            <li><strong>A machine matched to your use</strong> — a walking pad for walking, and a full
            treadmill if you intend to run.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">budget treadmills and walking pads worth buying</a>
            applies those tests to machines selling for $150 to $300, and explains which listing claims to
            ignore. If you are heavier and want a pad with more margin, the
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity walking pad roundup</a>
            covers pads with confirmed ratings from 330 to 400 lb.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a cheap treadmill last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No one publishes reliable lifespan data for budget treadmills or walking pads, so be wary of anyone quoting a number. Most carry about a one-year motor warranty, which tells you what the maker is prepared to stand behind. Light use, a good weight margin, rest between long sessions and regular lubrication make the biggest difference to how long one lasts in practice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I lubricate a cheap treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the interval in your manual, which is usually given in hours of use or months. A pad used for hours every working day reaches an hours-based interval far sooner than one used occasionally. Use the lubricant the manual specifies, usually 100% silicone, keep a dated note, and check the belt for dryness if the motor starts to sound strained.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a walking pad all day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many are marketed for desk use, but a small motor at steady load heats up over long periods. Check the manual for a maximum continuous running time; if it gives one, treat it as a limit. Otherwise break long days into blocks with genuine pauses, keep well under the weight rating, and stop if the motor area smells hot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my cheap treadmill keep overheating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually friction or overload. A dry deck, a belt tensioned too tight, dust around the motor, a user near the weight limit or very long sessions all make a small motor draw more current and run hot. Unplug it, let it cool, lubricate the deck, check the tension and clean around the motor before using it again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I plug a treadmill into a power strip?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Ordinary power strips and household extension cords are not designed for a treadmill motor's starting surge and varying load, and a long or thin cord can starve the motor of voltage. Plug directly into a grounded wall outlet, ideally on a circuit not shared with heavy appliances, and follow the manual on whether a surge protector is recommended.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth repairing a cheap treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cheap fixes are worth doing: lubricant, a belt adjustment, a replacement remote or safety key. A failed motor or control board out of warranty on a budget pad often costs a large share of the price of a new one, and is usually where repair stops making sense. Check whether the brand sells the part before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes That Shorten a Budget Treadmill’s Life</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">All four come from treating a machine with no margin as if it had some.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the printed capacity as a target</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A budget pad rated at 265 lb is at its limit with a 265 lb user, every step. That shows up as motor heat, belt wear and early board failure. Leave at least 50 lb of margin over the heaviest regular user, and if the listing and manual disagree on capacity, plan on the lower figure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a desk pad all day without a break</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A small motor heats up the longer it runs at load, and a desk day can be several hours. Check the manual for a session limit and respect it; otherwise break the day into blocks with genuine pauses. Let the machine cool before folding it or pushing it under furniture.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening the belt instead of lubricating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Slip on a budget machine is usually a dry deck, not a loose belt. Tightening presses the belt harder onto a deck that was already dragging, so friction and motor heat rise. Lubricate first with the specified silicone, then adjust tension in equal quarter-turns with the machine unplugged between checks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging into a power strip or long extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Power strips and household cords are not built for a treadmill motor's starting surge, and a long or thin cord drops the voltage, making the motor run hotter. Plug directly into a grounded wall outlet, avoid sharing a busy circuit, and follow the manual on surge protection.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

